#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""kilix-voiced — the one process that owns the microphone and the speakers.

Kilix's tab-bar buttons and the two TUIs are clients; this daemon is where a
read-aloud request becomes audio and a dictation request becomes text.  It
exists because the audio device has to have exactly one owner: half-duplex is
only enforceable if one process decides what is speaking and what is listening,
which is the policy ``voicelib.arbiter`` holds on its behalf.

Wire protocol: line-delimited JSON over ``<session>/control.sock``, an
``AF_UNIX``/``SOCK_SEQPACKET`` socket in a directory only this user can enter.
One request per connection, one reply, then the connection closes — a client
that holds a connection open cannot stall anybody else's request.  Every
accepted connection is checked with ``SO_PEERCRED``.

    {"op":"speak","text":"…"}        -> {"ok":true,"id":"","chunks":3}
    {"op":"stop-speech"}             -> {"ok":true,"id":"","stopped":true}
    {"op":"dictate","sock":"…"}      -> {"ok":true,"id":"","turn":"listen-1"}
    {"op":"stop-dictation"}          -> {"ok":true,"id":"","stopped":true,
                                         "quiesced":true,"mode":"finish"}
    {"op":"stop-dictation","mode":"abort"}
                                     -> {"ok":true,"id":"","stopped":true,
                                         "quiesced":true,"mode":"abort"}
    {"op":"status"}                  -> {"ok":true,"id":"","status":{…}}
    {"op":"ingest-audio"} + 1 fd     -> {"ok":true,"id":"","job":"ingest-1",
                                         "audio_fd":0,"pcm_bytes":…} + 1 fd

Dictation results go to the caller's socket as datagrams — ``{"partial":…}``
while the person is still speaking, one ``{"final":…}`` at the end, or
``{"error":…}`` — never back down the control connection, which has already
been answered and closed.

Nothing here is configured privately: every knob comes from the shared Kilix
settings document, re-read before each turn so a change made in ``kilix-tts``
applies to the next thing spoken.  Point ``GPU_TERMINAL_SETTINGS_FILE`` at
another document to move all of it at once.  ``--config`` (or
``KILIX_VOICE_CONFIG``) layers a JSON file over the result, which is how the
test suite injects fake capture and playback commands and a recogniser that
recognises nothing, on a machine with no audio at all:

    {"audio": {"capture_cmd": ["cat", "fixture.raw"],
               "play_cmd":    ["cat"]},
     "stt":   {"engine": "null"},
     "daemon": {"idle_seconds": 5}}

The daemon runs in the foreground and logs to stderr; whoever starts it decides
whether that is a terminal, a log file, or /dev/null.  Spoken and recognised
text is never logged — it is the user's, and stderr is not private.
"""

